> Layouts

the following layouts exist, most with multiple variants:

- dwindle
- scroller (horizontal/vertical)
- master/stack (called tile - left/right/vertical/center)
- monocle
- grid (horizontal/vertical)
- fair
- deck

this also covers the *overview* and *scratchpad*.

## Dwindle

binary space partitioning: each new window splits the focused window across its longest side.
wider than tall -> vertical split. taller than wide -> horizontal split.

special toggle *super+A*: maximizes the focused window (windowed, covering others).

## Scroller

horizontal and vertical variants.

windows laid out in an infinitely scrollable strip of columns.
you can vertically tile within a column.

special toggle *super+A*: changes the width of the focused scrolling column between presets.
scroll with *super+arrow keys* or mouse over partially-offscreen windows.

## Master/Stack (Tile)

left, right, vertical and center variants.

primary master window uses ~70% of the monitor. all other windows tile around it.

left/right: stack tiles vertically beside master.
vertical: stack tiles horizontally below master.
center: stack tiles alternating left and right of master.

special toggle *super+A*: swaps focused window with master.
drag and drop a window into master area to make it master.

## Grid

simple grid, all windows same size.
horizontal grid: left to right, then top to bottom.
vertical grid: top to bottom, then left to right.

special toggle *super+A*: toggles gaps and borders.

## Fair

similar to grid but fills the monitor as evenly as possible, no empty space.

## Monocle

one window at a time. cycle with *super+shift+left/right* or use overview.

special toggle does nothing.

## Deck

combination of monocle and master/stack: master window plus a monocle-style stack.

special toggle *super+A*: makes focused window the master.

## Overview

enter by moving mouse to top-left corner (hot corner) or holding alt+tab.

shows all windows on that monitor regardless of tag/workspace/layout.
tab to cycle, release alt to focus, left/right click to focus/close.

## Scratchpad

hidden space for frequently-accessed items (notepad, music player, terminal, file browser).

*super+C*: toggle visibility, cycle through multiple items.
*super+ctrl+C*: move window to scratchpad.
*super+alt+C*: move window out of scratchpad.

scratchpad windows float slightly smaller than your window for drag-and-drop.

SNAPS-OS also has a special terminal scratchpad (*super+alt+space*):
- toggles visibility if a terminal with the special title exists inside
- spawns one if it doesn't exist
- pre-loaded on primary monitor via autostart
